# A1/A1.1/A1_08_places_and_directions.letz
# Source: Quizlet "A1 Basics Beginner Luxembourgish Lesson 8"
#   https://quizlet.com/_h2svv7
# Comma-separated noun forms split; gender markers stripped;
# (no) and (ewech) optional words produce extra @lu variants;
# (to) optional EN endings produce extra @en variants.
# @fill exercises added: an+dative/accusative contrast, locative prepositions,
# direction words (lénks/riets/riichtaus), question words (Wou/Wuer).

@lesson A1.08 "Places & Directions"

# --- Place nouns (Quizlet cards 1-12) ---
@word d'Haus = house
@word Haiser = houses

@word d'Gebai = building
@word Gebaier = buildings

@word de Buttek = shop
@word Butteker = shops

@word d'Gemeng = commune
@word Gemengen = communes

@word den Theater = theatre
@word Theateren = theatres

@word d'Kierch = church
@word Kierchen = churches

@word d'Schoul = school
@word Schoulen = schools

@word d'Bréck = bridge
@word Brécken = bridges

@word de Kino = cinema
@word Kinoen = cinemas

@word d'Klinick = hospital
@word Klinicken = hospitals

@word d'Apdikt = pharmacy
@word Apdikten = pharmacies

@word de Café = café
@word Caféen = cafés

# --- Directional phrases (Quizlet cards 35-40) ---
@word laanscht de Kleederrayon = past the clothing aisle
@word de Rez-de-chaussée = the ground floor
@word den éischte Stack = the first floor
@word Richtung Mamer = in the direction of Mamer
@word op der rietser Säit = on the right hand side
@word op der lénker Säit = on the left hand side

# --- Vocabulary from sentences (coverage backfill) ---
@word de Park = park
@word d'Stad = city
@word d'Wollek = cloud
@word lénks = left
@word riets = right
@word riichtaus = straight ahead
@word wäit = far
@word hannert = behind
@word niewent = next to
@word tëschent = between

# --- Sentences (Quizlet cards 13-34) ---

@sentence
@lu De Restaurant ass niewent der Kierch.
@en The restaurant is next to the church.
@distractor-en school
@distractor-en bridge
@distractor-en behind
@distractor-lu Schoul
@distractor-lu Bréck
@distractor-lu hannert

@sentence
@lu D'Emma ass an der Schoul.
@en Emma is in school.
@distractor-en church
@distractor-en home
@distractor-lu Kierch
@distractor-lu doheem

@sentence
@lu De Rick steet tëschent zwee Beem.
@en Rick is standing between two trees.
@distractor-en hospital
@distractor-en pharmacy
@distractor-en behind
@distractor-lu Klinick
@distractor-lu Apdikt
@distractor-lu hannert

@sentence
@lu De Park ass no bei der Bank.
@lu De Park ass bei der Bank.
@en The park is close to the bank.
@distractor-en café
@distractor-en shop
@distractor-lu Café
@distractor-lu Buttek

@sentence
@lu D'Post ass wäit ewech vun der Bank.
@lu D'Post ass wäit vun der Bank.
@en The post office is far away from the bank.
@distractor-en building
@distractor-en commune
@distractor-lu Gebai
@distractor-lu Gemeng

@sentence
@lu De Café ass vis-à-vis vun der Apdikt.
@en The café is opposite the pharmacy.
@distractor-en theatre
@distractor-en church
@distractor-lu Theater
@distractor-lu Kierch

@sentence
@lu De Vic steet hannert der Bréck.
@en Vic is standing behind the bridge.
@distractor-en school
@distractor-en cinema
@distractor-en in front of
@distractor-lu Schoul
@distractor-lu Kino
@distractor-lu virun

@sentence
@lu D'Martine steet virun der Gemeng.
@en Martine is standing in front of the commune.
@distractor-en bridge
@distractor-en hospital
@distractor-en behind
@distractor-lu Bréck
@distractor-lu Klinick
@distractor-lu hannert

@sentence
@lu Ech sinn op der Post.
@en I'm at the post office.
@distractor-en theatre
@distractor-en church
@distractor-lu Theater
@distractor-lu Kierch

@sentence
@lu Ech sinn an de Wolleken.
@en My head is in the clouds.
@distractor-en city
@distractor-en building
@distractor-lu Stad
@distractor-lu Gebai

@sentence
@lu Ech ginn an den Theater.
@en I'm going to the theatre.
@distractor-en cinema
@distractor-en church
@distractor-lu Kino
@distractor-lu Kierch

@sentence
@lu Ech sinn am Theater.
@en I'm at the theatre.
@distractor-en cinema
@distractor-en café
@distractor-lu Kino
@distractor-lu Café

@sentence
@lu Ech ginn an d'Stad.
@en I'm going to the city.
@distractor-en building
@distractor-en come
@distractor-lu Gebai
@distractor-lu kommen

@sentence
@lu Ech sinn an der Stad.
@en I'm in the city.
@distractor-en building
@distractor-en church
@distractor-lu Gebai
@distractor-lu Kierch

@sentence
@lu Ech ginn an d'Gebai.
@en I'm going into the building.
@distractor-en city
@distractor-en out of
@distractor-lu Stad
@distractor-lu aus

@sentence
@lu Ech sinn am Gebai.
@en I'm in the building.
@distractor-en outside
@distractor-en city
@distractor-lu dobaussen
@distractor-lu Stad

@sentence
@lu Wou bass du?
@en Where are you?
@distractor-en how
@distractor-en when
@distractor-lu Wéi
@distractor-lu Wéini

@sentence
@lu Wuer fiers du?
@en Where are you driving?
@en Where are you driving to?
@distractor-en come
@distractor-en how
@distractor-lu kommen
@distractor-lu Wéi

@sentence
@lu Wuer gees du?
@en Where are you going?
@en Where are you going to?
@distractor-en come
@distractor-en when
@distractor-lu kommen
@distractor-lu Wéini

@sentence
@lu Géi lénks!
@en Go left!
@distractor-en right
@distractor-en straight
@distractor-lu riets
@distractor-lu riichtaus

@sentence
@lu Géi riets!
@en Go right!
@distractor-en left
@distractor-en straight
@distractor-lu lénks
@distractor-lu riichtaus

@sentence
@lu Géi riichtaus!
@en Go straight ahead!
@distractor-en left
@distractor-en right
@distractor-lu lénks
@distractor-lu riets

# --- @fill: an + dative (location) vs accusative (motion) -------------------
# Centrepiece of this lesson: "am/an der" (I am IN) vs "an d'/an den" (I go TO).
# Distractors swap the two contracted forms so the learner must pick the right case.

@fill
@lu D'Sophie ass [an der] Schoul.
@en Sophie is [in] school.
@distractor-lu an d'
@distractor-lu am
@distractor-lu op der
@distractor-en at
@distractor-en to
@distractor-en from

@fill
@lu De Tom geet [an d'] Schoul.
@en Tom goes [to] school.
@distractor-lu an der
@distractor-lu am
@distractor-lu op d'
@distractor-en in
@distractor-en from
@distractor-en at

@fill
@lu D'Sophie ass [am] Kino.
@en Sophie is [at] the cinema.
@distractor-lu an den
@distractor-lu an d'
@distractor-lu op dem
@distractor-en to
@distractor-en in the
@distractor-en from

@fill
@lu De Rick geet [an den] Kino.
@en Rick goes [to the] cinema.
@distractor-lu am
@distractor-lu an d'
@distractor-lu op den
@distractor-en at the
@distractor-en in the
@distractor-en from the

@fill
@lu D'Martine geet [an d'] Stad.
@en Martine goes [to the] city.
@distractor-lu an der
@distractor-lu am
@distractor-lu op d'
@distractor-en in the
@distractor-en at the
@distractor-en from the

@fill
@lu De Vic ass [an der] Stad.
@en Vic is [in the] city.
@distractor-lu an d'
@distractor-lu am
@distractor-lu op der
@distractor-en to the
@distractor-en at the
@distractor-en from the

# --- @fill: locative prepositions -------------------------------------------
# Each fill drills one preposition from the lesson's taught set. Distractors are
# other prepositions from the same set so the learner discriminates meaning.

@fill
@lu D'Schoul ass [niewent] der Kierch.
@en The school is [next to] the church.
@distractor-lu hannert
@distractor-lu virun
@distractor-lu tëschent
@distractor-en behind
@distractor-en in front of
@distractor-en between

@fill
@lu De Café ass [hannert] der Bréck.
@en The café is [behind] the bridge.
@distractor-lu virun
@distractor-lu niewent
@distractor-lu tëschent
@distractor-en in front of
@distractor-en next to
@distractor-en between

@fill
@lu De Kino ass [virun] der Gemeng.
@en The cinema is [in front of] the commune.
@distractor-lu hannert
@distractor-lu niewent
@distractor-lu tëschent
@distractor-en behind
@distractor-en next to
@distractor-en between

@fill
@lu D'Kierch ass [tëschent] der Post an der Apdikt.
@en The church is [between] the post office and the pharmacy.
@distractor-lu niewent
@distractor-lu hannert
@distractor-lu virun
@distractor-en next to
@distractor-en behind
@distractor-en in front of

@fill
@lu D'Apdikt ass [vis-à-vis] vun der Kierch.
@en The pharmacy is [opposite] the church.
@distractor-lu niewent
@distractor-lu hannert
@distractor-lu virun
@distractor-en next to
@distractor-en behind
@distractor-en in front of

@fill
@lu De Buttek ass [wäit] vun der Schoul.
@en The shop is [far] from the school.
@distractor-lu no
@distractor-lu niewent
@distractor-lu hannert
@distractor-en close
@distractor-en next
@distractor-en behind

# --- @fill: directions (lénks / riets / riichtaus) --------------------------
# The imperative frame is already drilled by @sentence, so these fills use a
# different subject or add context to avoid duplication.

@fill
@lu D'Sophie geet [lénks] an de Park.
@en Sophie goes [left] to the park.
@distractor-lu riets
@distractor-lu riichtaus
@distractor-lu hannert
@distractor-en right
@distractor-en straight ahead
@distractor-en behind

@fill
@lu De Tom geet [riets] bei de Buttek.
@en Tom goes [right] towards the shop.
@distractor-lu lénks
@distractor-lu riichtaus
@distractor-lu virun
@distractor-en left
@distractor-en straight ahead
@distractor-en in front of

# --- @fill: question words (Wou / Wuer) ------------------------------------
# Wou = where (location), Wuer = where to (direction). The learner must pick the
# right question word for the context.

@fill
@lu [Wou] ass de Buttek?
@en [Where] is the shop?
@distractor-lu Wuer
@distractor-lu Wéi
@distractor-lu Wéini
@distractor-en When
@distractor-en How
@distractor-en What

@fill
@lu [Wuer] gees du muer?
@en [Where] are you going tomorrow?
@distractor-lu Wou
@distractor-lu Wéi
@distractor-lu Wéini
@distractor-en When
@distractor-en How
@distractor-en What
